The note states that approximable mappings are exactly the maps of elements that preserve unions of chains when the information system is countable, and directed unions in general, equivalently the continuous maps of the element spaces, and then Proposition 5.4: the relation \(u\,I_{\mathbf{A}}\,v\) if and only if \(u\vdash_{\mathbf{A}} v\), for consistent finite \(u\) and \(v\), defines an approximable mapping, and \(I_{\mathbf{A}}(x)=x\) for every element \(x\). `idMap` defines that relation by entailment and checks the approximable-mapping axioms: the empty sets are related because the empty set is consistent and entails the empty set, union on the right uses entailment of a union together with consistency of a set with the finite set it entails, and monotonicity is transitivity of entailment. `idMap.toElement` proves \(I_{\mathbf{A}}(x)=x\): a token of the image is entailed by a finite subset of \(x\) and therefore lies in the closure of \(x\), while a token of \(x\) has its singleton contained in \(x\) and reflexivity of entailment relates that singleton to itself. `Proposition54` records the element equation as `theorem_54`. `Basic` states both the identification of the identity relation with entailment and the element equation. Clauses (i) and (ii) match. The chain-union and topological characterization in the lead-in is not proved. **Theorem:** partial — (i) and (ii) hold via `idMap` and `idMap.toElement`, while preservation of chain unions is absent. **Proof:** partial — the entailment checks and the two inclusions for \(I_{\mathbf{A}}(x)=x\) are proved, and there is no argument for the chain-union characterization.
